{
  "description": "A forked Codex rollout, as the historical importer reads it. A fork under ForkPersistence::Copied re-writes the parent's entire history into the child's file in one call, and Codex stamps every record it serializes with the write time -- so the first three lines here share one millisecond. Line 1 is the child's OWN session_meta, written at the moment the fork happened, so its timestamp is a true event time and it carries no time_basis. Lines 2 and 3 are the parent's records, copied in behind it, whose original times are no longer recoverable: they are marked copied_at. Line 4 is the child's own live turn, two hours later. All four are imported; the marking is what keeps two of them off every clock without losing them.",
  "exercises": [
    "copied history is marked, never dropped -- a copied human prompt still reaches the ledger",
    "time_basis = copied_at on every observation derived from a copied record",
    "the rule is upstream's own: a session_meta whose payload.id is not the file's canonical thread id",
    "the fork's own session_meta is live: it is when the fork happened, not a copy of anything",
    "the child's own live turn carries no time_basis, because its timestamp is when it happened",
    "an embedded parent session_meta names a session this file registers nothing for, so it gaps -- and the gap is copied-dated too"
  ],
